WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:03.060
- How beautiful is this?

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:05.040
This is an experiment in quantum mechanics

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.930
called the double slit experiment

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:10.290
but I really didn't expect to
see this when I did it myself.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:13.830
And seeing this changed my
mind on an important question

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:17.010
in physics, what is light?

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.120
But before all that,

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.130
here's a simple experiment
that you can do.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:24.183
All you'll need is a laser
pointer and a strand of hair.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.840
So if I just take this
laser and I shine it

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:32.430
at this whiteboard here,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.650
you can see that you just get a dot.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.510
But what do you reckon will happen

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:39.270
if I put this hair in the way?

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.480
- You might expect the dot
to get split into two dots

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:45.420
or for there to be a
dark line through the dot

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.213
or nothing at all to happen
since the hair is so small.

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:52.410
But let me show you what
actually happens in the dark

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.723
because it looks really impressive.

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:57.630
Surprising, right?

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.640
And it's nothing to do
with the hair itself.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.420
You can do the exact same
experiment with anything thin.

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:06.000
This is a variation on
the double slit experiment

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:08.160
which is an experiment
that's so important,

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:10.410
you learn about it of pretty much day one

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.810
of any quantum 101 class.

00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:15.300
The normal way to do the
experiment is to shine a light

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:17.160
on two very narrow slits.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:18.360
Then the light that goes through,

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:20.730
goes through the left or to the right.

00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:22.800
This variation is pretty much the same.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.050
The light goes through
one way or the other.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.420
But besides being easier to do this way,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.850
the other advantage is
the pattern you get.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.670
You can see that they both
have the same sort of flavor

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:34.170
but they're different.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:36.690
For example, the dashes of
light for the hair version

00:01:36.690 --> 00:01:39.930
are all evenly spaced
and get lighter as we go

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:43.320
but the dots of the double
slit are much more patchy.

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.690
That's because of something
called single slit interference

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:48.810
which is basically a
distraction in this scenario.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:51.660
So yeah, I kinda prefer the hair version.

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:53.490
Plus check this out.

00:01:53.490 --> 00:01:58.410
I'm using this smoke in a can
stuff to see the actual beam

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.530
but look really closely at the beam

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.770
and you can actually see
where the hair splits

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.360
the light into two.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:08.010
How crazy is that?

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:11.760
And looking at this, I think
it's really hard to understand

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:13.740
why you get the crazy double slip pattern

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:17.220
when it seems like obviously
you should just get a dot

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.370
with a line through it.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.350
So I've done my PhD in quantum stuff

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.080
and I thought I understood
the theory of this experiment

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:28.200
really well and so my plan
was just to explain it to you

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:29.160
in a cute little video

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:31.680
about how to do the experiment at home.

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.710
Instead, I got sucked into an odyssey

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:37.590
for the last few months where
I did all kinds of variations

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.290
on the experiment and I
ended up like questioning

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.840
pretty much everything I
thought I knew about light.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.900
It turns out this experiment
is just way more interesting

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:46.733
than I thought.

00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:48.570
There's always been this debate in physics

00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:51.750
about whether light is made
out of a stream of particles

00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:54.810
or light is some sort of
excitation like a wave.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.360
The double slit experiment
was meant to definitively

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.290
prove that light is a wave
except later experiments

00:03:01.290 --> 00:03:02.580
seem to show that light

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:04.653
has particle light properties as well.

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.280
I used to hate this
whole wave particle thing

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.920
because light is clearly neither.

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.180
A great analogy I heard for it

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:13.890
is that light is like a platypus,

00:03:13.890 --> 00:03:16.770
it doesn't make sense to
say that it's both a duck

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:20.040
and a beaver at the same time
because it is its own thing.

00:03:20.040 --> 00:03:22.890
But actually doing this
experiment finally made me see

00:03:22.890 --> 00:03:24.720
the value in having good analogies

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.280
to help you think about light.

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:28.890
And in particular, it really
sold me on thinking about

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:30.570
light in terms of waves.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:34.290
It also made me see how
misleading the particle analogy

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.290
can be if you're not
super careful about it.

00:03:37.290 --> 00:03:40.560
I think many of us imagine
photons which are what physicists

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:43.230
now call particles of
light in a similar way

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:45.720
to how Newton thought of light particles.

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.550
But the double slit experiment shows why

00:03:47.550 --> 00:03:50.730
Newton's particle view
is definitely wrong.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.910
Newton imagined light in the
same way we now think of water.

00:03:53.910 --> 00:03:56.790
Inside it's just made of a
stream of light particles

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:00.150
in the same way that water is
made out of water particles.

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:01.470
I'm gonna call these particles

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:03.390
that Newton thought light was made out of

00:04:03.390 --> 00:04:06.390
localized particles because
at every point in time

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.250
they're just in one spot in space.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:09.690
They're not in multiple places

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.420
or very spread out or anything like that.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.250
If you turn down the laser enough,

00:04:14.250 --> 00:04:16.470
Newton would've expected
that light comes out

00:04:16.470 --> 00:04:18.720
in these individual particles.

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.510
Instead, people like Thomas
Young imagine light coming out

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:23.940
as some sort of wave.

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.000
In this case, no matter how
much you turn down the laser,

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:28.770
the light would still be a wave,

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.530
it would just be less and less wavy.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:33.600
The light travels forward not in lines

00:04:33.600 --> 00:04:36.393
but as peaks of a wave
progressing forward.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.720
In the case of just
turning on a flashlight,

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:43.020
both Newton's picture and
Thomas Young's wave picture

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:45.060
make exactly the same predictions.

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:48.330
But Newton's view just feels right

00:04:48.330 --> 00:04:49.560
like you have these particles

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:52.620
just traveling in straight
lines which is so much simpler

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.440
than this strange abstract wave thing

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.530
which is why it's so tempting
to think of light this way.

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:01.320
In fact, I've seen
pictures just like this one

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.990
to illustrate photons
exiting a light source

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:07.110
in many reputable textbooks and I get why,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:09.120
that's kind of how I imagine photons too.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:12.150
So how did Thomas Young
go about disproving it?

00:05:12.150 --> 00:05:14.880
He needed to find an experiment
that could only be explained

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:17.460
with waves and not with
Newton's particles.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:19.650
Well, one unique thing that waves do

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.930
is that they bend and spread out.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:24.000
You may have seen this
before but for me at least,

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.380
it was kinda surprising.

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:28.503
Let me show you with this high
tech ripple tank I made here.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.220
You can see that when I bob
this plastic in the water,

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:34.830
it makes fairly straight waves.

00:05:34.830 --> 00:05:36.270
But what do you expect will happen

00:05:36.270 --> 00:05:39.090
when I put this obstruction in the way?

00:05:39.090 --> 00:05:40.710
I would've guessed that
the bit of the wave

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:43.290
that makes it past just
goes straight ahead

00:05:43.290 --> 00:05:46.260
and that's what the
majority of the wave does do

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:49.920
but the rest of it bends and spreads out.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.940
You can see that in action
here but why does it happen?

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:54.870
I don't really know.

00:05:54.870 --> 00:05:55.980
I would love to understand it better

00:05:55.980 --> 00:05:58.110
and make a video about it but until then,

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:01.230
you can see that this is
something waves actually do.

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:03.660
They spread out when
they meet an obstacle.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:05.340
That definitely feels a lot different

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:07.980
from how I'd expect particles to behave.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:10.500
So why don't we do a similar
experiment with light

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:11.940
and see what happens?

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:15.750
Will light bend and spread
out or will it go straight?

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.220
I'm gonna use a piece of paper

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.320
as the obstruction to find out.

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:20.490
And there you go.

00:06:20.490 --> 00:06:23.070
The laser light does actually spread out.

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:25.800
Case closed, light is a wave, right?

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:29.310
Well, maybe we should give
Newton a chance to rebut this.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:32.310
I guess he could try and
explain this with particles.

00:06:32.310 --> 00:06:35.190
Perhaps the particles are
colliding into the paper

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:36.480
and then ricocheting

00:06:36.480 --> 00:06:38.700
and that's why they spread out like this.

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:39.533
Okay, fine.

00:06:39.533 --> 00:06:41.310
Maybe you can explain that.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.230
But there is another wave phenomenon

00:06:43.230 --> 00:06:46.110
that particles definitely can't mimic

00:06:46.110 --> 00:06:47.670
and that's canceling out.

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:49.590
One way to think of the last experiment

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:52.320
is that it's like we
took the hair experiment

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:54.540
and then just blocked one path.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.640
We found that at least
some of the light ends up

00:06:56.640 --> 00:07:00.360
in all of these places
including in this spot.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.550
If we had blocked the right path instead,

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.800
we'd have gotten a very similar result.

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:07.140
And again, notice that at
least some of the light

00:07:07.140 --> 00:07:09.330
falls on this spot.

00:07:09.330 --> 00:07:12.360
Now that's open both paths simultaneously

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:15.660
and the light just
disappears from this spot.

00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:18.660
There is no way for nu to explain this.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:19.650
Think about it.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:22.320
If you believe in the kind
of particles that Newton did,

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.720
then when just the left path is open,

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:26.820
some of your particles
will reach that spot

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:28.920
and when the right path is open,

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:31.080
some of your particles
will reach that spot.

00:07:31.080 --> 00:07:34.500
But when both of them are
open, it's like the particles

00:07:34.500 --> 00:07:37.860
from the right side and the
particles from the left side

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:41.280
just happened to cancel
each other out in this spot

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.440
which just doesn't make sense.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:44.273
But why not?

00:07:44.273 --> 00:07:46.830
Why can't you just have
the particles cancel?

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:49.470
Well, because in this
spot they need to cancel

00:07:49.470 --> 00:07:51.180
but just a little bit over,

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:54.030
the particles coming from
the left and from the right

00:07:54.030 --> 00:07:55.920
should happily coincide.

00:07:55.920 --> 00:07:58.050
It's like they sometimes choose to cancel

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:00.060
and sometimes they choose not to.

00:08:00.060 --> 00:08:03.900
So I'm sorry Newton,
but particles like yours

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:05.520
really aren't good enough to explain

00:08:05.520 --> 00:08:07.710
how you could ever get canceling.

00:08:07.710 --> 00:08:10.200
But then how do waves manage to explain

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.460
the double slit experiment?

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:14.160
Let's imagine a wave is
coming out of the laser.

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:16.380
On the right side, the
wave encounters the hair

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.030
and because it's an obstacle,

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:21.300
it bends and it spreads out
like we discussed before.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:23.853
On the other side, the
exact same thing happens.

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:25.980
Back in our wave pool,

00:08:25.980 --> 00:08:29.430
the black obstruction in the
middle now represents the hair.

00:08:29.430 --> 00:08:32.250
And as you can see, the waves
on either side do spread out

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:33.570
and they overlap.

00:08:33.570 --> 00:08:36.720
But something very surprising
is happening in that overlap

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.710
and you can't really make it
out in this precision setup

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:43.473
but it's gonna be a lot
more clear in the animation.

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:47.670
Where are all these lines coming from?

00:08:47.670 --> 00:08:48.990
To understand what's going on,

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:51.270
imagine you're on a boat in this spot.

00:08:51.270 --> 00:08:53.820
Then the wave coming from
the left will have its peak

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:56.400
reach you at exactly the same time

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:58.770
as the wave coming from the right.

00:08:58.770 --> 00:09:01.740
Together they tend to
push you up even higher.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:04.080
The troughs also meet you at the same time

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:06.690
so this spot is just really choppy.

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:09.090
But if we instead move our boat to here,

00:09:09.090 --> 00:09:11.250
then the peak of the
right wave hits exactly

00:09:11.250 --> 00:09:13.560
when the trough of the left wave hits you.

00:09:13.560 --> 00:09:15.420
The peak tries to move you upward

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:17.400
while the trough tries to pull you down.

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:19.500
And so in the end, they cancel out

00:09:19.500 --> 00:09:21.720
and the boat doesn't
really move up or down.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.510
So this spot is surprisingly calm.

00:09:24.510 --> 00:09:27.600
There are these choppy
and calm spots all over

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:31.140
and if you map them out,
then you get these lines.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:33.300
What's this got to do with
the double slit experiment?

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:36.090
Well, look at the waves across the wall.

00:09:36.090 --> 00:09:39.810
You have these alternating
parts of choppiness and calmness

00:09:39.810 --> 00:09:41.880
that lines up suspiciously well

00:09:41.880 --> 00:09:43.500
with the pattern of the double slit

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:46.710
where you have alternating
spots of light and dark.

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:48.690
Thomas Young's hypothesis was,

00:09:48.690 --> 00:09:50.980
when there's this waving, there's light

00:09:52.050 --> 00:09:54.660
and when the waves cancel
out and so there's no waving,

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:55.800
there is no light.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:58.530
In other words, the amount
of light produced in a spot

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:00.723
is proportional to the amount of waving.

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:05.400
You know, I've given this
explanation to students for years

00:10:05.400 --> 00:10:08.550
but I've never taken the whole
wave picture very seriously.

00:10:08.550 --> 00:10:11.340
Like yes, I knew that to
make the right prediction

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:12.450
for the double slit,

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:15.090
you would have to use
waves in your calculation.

00:10:15.090 --> 00:10:16.890
But I thought they were
just that, you know?

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:18.990
Like just calculation tools.

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:20.490
I didn't think that they were real.

00:10:20.490 --> 00:10:22.620
I mean, like literally
in quantum mechanics,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.630
the waves are complex valued.

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:28.740
So instead in my head, I thought
of the light as a particle

00:10:28.740 --> 00:10:32.040
when it exited the laser
and then a particle again

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:33.758
when it got measured at
the wall but in between

00:10:33.758 --> 00:10:36.930
it just seemed like there's
nothing you could really say

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:40.080
about light besides a bunch of equations.

00:10:40.080 --> 00:10:42.990
But this next experiment
is what changed my mind

00:10:42.990 --> 00:10:47.820
and made me finally feel that
the waves are actually real.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:49.290
So I've been playing with this for a while

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:52.680
and I haven't been able to get
this thought out of my mind.

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:57.680
There's a beam here that
leads to this middle dot,

00:10:58.140 --> 00:11:00.990
that's clearly where
the light for that dot

00:11:00.990 --> 00:11:02.430
is coming from, right?

00:11:02.430 --> 00:11:04.593
But what about all these other dots?

00:11:05.460 --> 00:11:08.670
Like surely that light is
coming from somewhere as well

00:11:08.670 --> 00:11:11.280
and it feels like you
can almost trace back

00:11:11.280 --> 00:11:13.500
exactly where that light's from.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:15.753
For this one, if I follow it,

00:11:20.250 --> 00:11:22.023
I can go all the way back.

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:24.933
And same for these as well.

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:29.850
So it feels like there should be beams

00:11:29.850 --> 00:11:32.823
kinda coming out here.

00:11:34.230 --> 00:11:38.040
I got obsessed with this idea
that you should be able to see

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:41.220
these beams of light if you
just use the right method.

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:45.510
I tried everything like doing
the experiment at midnight

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:49.860
for maximum contrast and then
filling a box with smoke.

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:52.380
Then I tried filling a
tub with water vapor.

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:57.033
I even tried submerging my
laser into aga, nothing.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:01.110
In the end I caved and
I bought a smoke machine

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:04.290
from a DJ shop and I also decided to use

00:12:04.290 --> 00:12:07.380
the proper double slit
so that the little bands

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:11.490
are more intense and it worked.

00:12:11.490 --> 00:12:13.920
I would love if you actually
tried this experiment yourself

00:12:13.920 --> 00:12:15.840
because it's so easy to do

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:17.790
but I need to warn you about something.

00:12:17.790 --> 00:12:20.640
Laser pointers are way less
safe than you probably think.

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:22.470
I bought a bunch of lasers from Amazon

00:12:22.470 --> 00:12:24.450
that all claim to be
under five milliwatts.

00:12:24.450 --> 00:12:27.030
Anything over that limit
can permanently burn a hole

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:27.863
in your retina.

00:12:27.863 --> 00:12:29.850
So let's compare these cheap lasers

00:12:29.850 --> 00:12:34.110
with a proper five milliwatt
laser to see if they're safe.

00:12:34.110 --> 00:12:37.350
As you can see, this green
one is definitely not okay.

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:38.820
In general, green lasers are something

00:12:38.820 --> 00:12:41.190
I really don't recommend
you buy from Amazon.

00:12:41.190 --> 00:12:43.290
Red laser pointers are
better in my experience

00:12:43.290 --> 00:12:45.690
especially little ones like
this that are advertised

00:12:45.690 --> 00:12:49.860
as like key chains or cat toys
rather than like this type.

00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:53.160
But yeah, if you're going to
be using a laser for presenting

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.080
or experiments or playing with your cat,

00:12:55.080 --> 00:12:57.420
just make sure it isn't
one that's going to burn

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:58.800
someone's eyes out.

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:02.021
Anyway, it was worth
buying the smoke machine.

00:13:02.021 --> 00:13:06.480
This experiment looks
just magical in real life

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:08.550
and all I've done to
capture it is use my phone,

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:09.990
nothing fancy.

00:13:09.990 --> 00:13:11.940
I set the double set up a few meters away

00:13:11.940 --> 00:13:15.330
and blocked all the lights and
turned on the smoke machine

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:18.123
and all of these beams
suddenly became visible.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:21.780
Here you can see that
there are beams of light

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:24.780
going to all of the dots
of the double slit pattern.

00:13:24.780 --> 00:13:26.310
But to see it even more clearly,

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:29.610
let's turn around and
look toward the laser.

00:13:29.610 --> 00:13:33.540
And now so many beams become visible.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:37.140
The double slit doesn't split
the light up into two beams,

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:40.950
it splits the light into many, many beams.

00:13:40.950 --> 00:13:44.370
So why was this experiment
such a revelation for me?

00:13:44.370 --> 00:13:47.580
Well, it felt like I was actually
seeing the light be a wave

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:48.450
for a first time.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:50.790
Let me show you what I
mean with this laser.

00:13:50.790 --> 00:13:52.830
Here you can see the
narrow beam of the laser

00:13:52.830 --> 00:13:55.020
but if I put this paper in the way,

00:13:55.020 --> 00:13:57.360
a wave should spread out

00:13:57.360 --> 00:13:59.850
and yeah, that's exactly
what the laser does.

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:02.373
But to see it more clearly,
let's add some smoke.

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:07.650
This looks almost unreal.

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:11.100
The laser beam is spread out
but only in this one plane

00:14:11.100 --> 00:14:13.020
just like we'd predict for a wave.

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:16.410
If I remove the paper and put
in the double slit instead,

00:14:16.410 --> 00:14:18.990
you can see the two sides
of the beam interfere

00:14:18.990 --> 00:14:22.893
and cancel out again,
just like a wave should.

00:14:25.350 --> 00:14:27.570
Seeing this was the first
time that I actually

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:31.080
felt in my gut that light
is somehow wavelike.

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:32.850
Up until now, I just
thought that the waves

00:14:32.850 --> 00:14:36.420
of quantum mechanics were
abstract and they're not.

00:14:36.420 --> 00:14:37.800
They're actually there

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:40.680
and you can see pretty
clear evidence of them.

00:14:40.680 --> 00:14:43.500
And so since then I've started
taking the whole wave picture

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:45.540
much more seriously.

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:48.360
On the other hand, I've
become more cautious

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:50.460
about imagining light as particles.

00:14:50.460 --> 00:14:54.060
Photons are much more nuanced
clearly than I thought before

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:56.340
and I wanna try and do
experiments with them as well

00:14:56.340 --> 00:14:58.710
to understand how I
should be thinking of them

00:14:58.710 --> 00:15:00.810
which is why quantum experiments at home

00:15:00.810 --> 00:15:02.550
is gonna be a series.

00:15:02.550 --> 00:15:04.620
I hope you have lots of
questions left about light

00:15:04.620 --> 00:15:06.720
and if you do, please
leave them in the comments

00:15:06.720 --> 00:15:09.840
and we'll try and sort them out
together in future episodes.

00:15:09.840 --> 00:15:10.673
See you then.

